=== DESIGN ===

D1. Migration 000189 — adds the surrogate id column required by
    Decision #3's `WHERE id=$1` clause.

    Decision #3 says `UPDATE ... WHERE id=$1`. Decision #4 lists
    only `acknowledged_at` and `acknowledged_by` as new columns —
    NOT `id`. The natural PK is (vehicle_id, ts, event_type) which
    cannot be addressed by a single REST URL token. The frontend
    type at web/src/api/hooks/useGuard.ts:19 explicitly declares
    `id: number` on GuardEvent. Adding an `id BIGINT` column is the
    only consistent interpretation. Documented as "Decision #4
    extended to add id BIGINT" in the migration's header comment.

    TimescaleDB constraint: a UNIQUE INDEX on (id) alone is rejected
    by hypertables that don't include the partition key. We use a
    NON-UNIQUE (vehicle_id, id) lookup index — the sequence
    guarantees practical uniqueness, and every UPDATE/SELECT path
    also filters by vehicle_id so a hypothetical sequence collision
    couldn't cross vehicles.

    Hypertable rewrite avoidance: BIGSERIAL with a volatile DEFAULT
    triggers a full rewrite of every chunk on existing rows. Two-
    step (ADD COLUMN BIGINT → SET DEFAULT nextval → backfill
    UPDATE WHERE id IS NULL → SET NOT NULL) avoids the rewrite.

D2. Acknowledge SQL (Decision #3 verbatim):

      UPDATE security_events
      SET acknowledged_at = now(),
          acknowledged_by = $3
      WHERE id = $1
        AND vehicle_id = $2
      RETURNING id, vehicle_id, ts, event_type, from_state, to_state,
                details, acknowledged_at, acknowledged_by

    Re-acknowledgement overwrites (Decision #3 says `SET
    acknowledged_at = now()`, NOT COALESCE). Cross-vehicle attempts
    return RowsAffected==0 → handler responds 404 with a generic
    "guard event not found" message (no auth side-channel).

D3. Rubber-duck-driven adjustments (consulted before implementation):

    [adopted] Drop UNIQUE INDEX(id) — TimescaleDB rejects it.
              → use non-unique (vehicle_id, id) lookup index.
    [adopted] Panic ≠ rate-limit. Rate-limit alone is too weak for a
              destructive command that wakes the car. Use RequireSudo
              for panic; rate-limit ALSO at 5/min on the same route.
              Acknowledge stays rate-limited only (mark-read soft write).
    [adopted] Don't insert a panic audit row in security_events.
              security_events is the telemetry-derived table; mixing
              user-initiated commands corrupts every analytic that
              consumes it. A future command_log surface is the right
              home; out-of-scope here.
    [adopted] Panic sends sentry_on → honk_horn → flash_lights, NOT
              just honk+flash. sentry_on FIRST so the vehicle starts
              recording before the audible/visible alerts. Partial
              failure handled per-command (continue + 502 with detail).
    [adopted] Treat Idle as ACTIVE sentry. SentryModeStateIdle means
              "Sentry is enabled but the vehicle isn't currently
              alarming". Only Off and Unknown count as inactive.
    [adopted] Empty actor allowed. actorFromRequest returns "" in
              open-mode; pass through verbatim, do NOT fabricate
              "system"/"anonymous".

D4. Response envelopes — match phase-43a/0003-0005 precedent:
    Status:  bare object with vehicle_id + sentry fields.
    Events:  envelope { vehicle_id, events: [...] } so the frontend's
             `select: (data) => safeArray(data?.events)` extraction
             pattern works.
    Ack:     bare GuardEvent (the updated row).
    Panic:   { vehicle_id, vin, results: [{command, ok, error?}] }.
             200 on full success, 502 on any per-command failure
             (with all 3 results present so the SPA can show what
             worked).

